WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.976 --> 00:00:08.100
MIGUEL HERNAN: Cigarette smoking is
one of the most important global health

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:09.210
problems.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.910
The World Health Organization
estimates that tobacco

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:14.580
kills about 6 million people each year.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.130
Cigarette smoking has a
causal effect on many cancers

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:21.760
and heart disease, stroke,
respiratory diseases, you name it.

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:23.910
But everybody knows this, right?

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:29.100
And yet for decades cigarette ads
showed tough, healthy men smoking.

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.100
But reality is something
hard to argue against.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.000
At least four Marlboro men died
of smoking related diseases.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.750
And some well-known Hollywood
actors also advertised cigarettes.

00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.830
One died of cancer,
the other of dementia,

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:46.350
which begs the question: does
smoking have a causal effect

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.300
on the risk of dementia.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:50.520
There are biological
reasons why smoking may

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:53.980
increase the risk of at
least some forms of dementia.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:57.880
However, we will never have a
randomized trial of cigarette smoking.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:00.920
Fortunately, such unethical
trial is not allowed.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:04.319
That means that we'll have to
rely on observational data only.

00:01:04.319 --> 00:01:06.960
In fact, several observational
prospective studies

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.800
of smoking and dementia have
been conducted since the 1990s.

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.140
However, the findings of
these observational studies

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:14.640
vary dramatically.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.990
Some studies found a higher risk of
dementia in smokers than in nonsmokers.

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:20.910
Other studies found the opposite.

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:24.030
Can we interpret any of these
associations as the effect of smoking

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.320
on dementia?

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:26.820
And if so, what's the answer?

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:29.550
Does smoking cause or prevent dementia?

00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:33.459
And most importantly, why do
results differ across studies?

00:01:33.459 --> 00:01:35.250
One possibility is
uncontrolled confounding

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:38.910
in some of the studies,
for example, by alcohol.

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:43.020
People who become cigarette smokers
also tend to drink more alcohol.

00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:45.270
If alcohol increases
the risk of dementia,

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.360
then we would expect to find
an association between smoking

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:52.290
and dementia, even if smoking
had no effect on dementia.

00:01:52.290 --> 00:01:55.710
But it looks like many observational
studies of smoking and dementia

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:58.900
did adjust for the most
important confounders.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:01.980
Then why did the smokers have a higher
risk of dementia in some studies

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:04.770
and a lower risk of
dementia in other studies?

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.190
Let me give you a hint.

00:02:06.190 --> 00:02:09.240
Perhaps it has to do with
the selection of individuals

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:10.820
in each of these studies.

00:02:10.820 --> 00:02:13.800
We will return to this problem
at the end of this lesson.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:17.840
And by then you will be able to
explain what's going on here.

